          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The axiom of
            %strong countable choice
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CC
            ), also called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AC
                    %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_\omega
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AC
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC_N
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %em every
            set is projective.)
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            the situation is more subtle. For varying reasons, some schools of constructive mathematics accept countable choice (though they reject the full axiom of choice). On the other hand, countable choice is not valid in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a general topos, so if one desires this level of generality then it should not be assumed. There are also philosophical constructivist arguments against it.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Richman"} Fred Richman
            %a{:href => "#RichmanFTA"} (RichmanFTA)
            has said that
          %blockquote
            %p Countable choice is a blind spot for constructive mathematicians in much the same way as excluded middle is for classical mathematicians.
          %p
            All the reasoning in this page is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#in_set_theory In set theory
          %p
